  {
    "episode_id": 77296,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/7/77296.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 69,
    "turn_text": "So after child separation ended, the administration asked for Congress to approve and provide authority to establish a massive expansion of family internment camps, walk up families together. And that is actually something that passed the House of Representatives. It had 35 Republican sponsors in the Senate. I went to the floor. I laid out the history of internment camps, the shameful chapter in World War II with Japanese Americans, and said this cannot be where America goes. And I introduced a counter bill called no a tournament camps in america act just the fact that one would have to introduce such legislation should put chills down our our spine that ended for all practical purposes the legislative strategy of the administration now they've turned to an executive strategy they have not only three family internment camps they also have fifteen thousand children locked up in child prison camps this is just something not getting the attention it deserves this is not In the tradition of America, it is not treating people, children, and parents with respect and dignity as they await asylum hearings. And certainly, America with our immigration history should treat people with respect and dignity.",
